[SPEAKER_04]: Even more importantly, recognize that if you don't have the right view, eh... nothing you do partakes in the right view.

186
00:15:20,494 --> 00:15:21,614
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's top to bottom.

187
00:15:21,774 --> 00:15:23,555
[SPEAKER_04]: You're enclosed within your wrong view.

188
00:15:24,316 --> 00:15:25,636
[SPEAKER_01]: It needs to be taken into that.

189
00:15:25,716 --> 00:15:29,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, there is some kind of contradiction, eh...

190
00:15:30,699 --> 00:15:34,721
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, present in my experience now, that there is some kind of...

191
00:15:36,110 --> 00:15:45,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, with my thoughts or my experience that there is, yeah, some... there is a lack of understanding.

192
00:15:45,598 --> 00:15:47,119
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah.

193
00:15:47,459 --> 00:15:48,440
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's kind of...

194
00:15:49,481 --> 00:15:52,983
[SPEAKER_04]: If you admit that you don't have the right view, yeah, then yeah, so there is lack of understanding.

195
00:15:53,504 --> 00:16:00,670
[SPEAKER_04]: But what I was trying to say is that it's important to really admit that you don't have the right view top to bottom,

196
00:16:01,662 --> 00:16:05,983
[SPEAKER_04]: Because if you are... if you leave that kind of ambiguous, it's like, yeah, I don't have the right view.

197
00:16:06,644 --> 00:16:15,487
[SPEAKER_04]: You could still cultivate the attitude as if I don't have the right view, but the stuff I'm doing surely must be partaking to the right view and getting me closer to it.

198
00:16:15,587 --> 00:16:16,667
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't know even that.

199
00:16:17,367 --> 00:16:20,248
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't know if it's taking you closer or further away from it.

200
00:16:20,808 --> 00:16:24,430
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that's the whole point of not having the right view means not having the right reference.

201
00:16:25,030 --> 00:16:27,871
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, you can't trust what you're doing.

202
00:16:28,935 --> 00:16:31,276
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if it feels good or right, fine.

203
00:16:32,856 --> 00:16:38,898
[SPEAKER_04]: Don't use the criteria of how it feels for whether it's right or wrong.

204
00:16:38,978 --> 00:16:42,720
[SPEAKER_04]: Use the information that you're gathering on how, you know, what the right view is supposed to be.

205
00:16:43,100 --> 00:16:44,760
[SPEAKER_04]: What the right view is supposed to be resulting.

206
00:16:45,201 --> 00:16:47,942
[SPEAKER_04]: What the practice along the right lines should be resulting in.

207
00:16:48,642 --> 00:16:52,983
[SPEAKER_04]: Not how it feels, but whether it's, you know, increasing the lust or decreasing it.

208
00:16:53,023 --> 00:16:54,624
[SPEAKER_04]: Increasing aversion or decreasing it.

209
00:16:55,814 --> 00:17:05,236
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you able to suffer when amidst suffering, as much as before, or amidst the stuff that used to make you suffer a lot, you're not as overwhelmed.

210
00:17:09,838 --> 00:17:17,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I was gonna say, like, you also... because you can kind of just take that as a... okay, I don't have the right view, I don't...

211
00:17:17,920 --> 00:17:21,001
[SPEAKER_00]: I must sort of doubt everything.

212
00:17:23,247 --> 00:17:30,195
[SPEAKER_00]: But there has to be some criteria that you can even verify within your own experience as having the wrong view to see.

213
00:17:30,515 --> 00:17:31,836
[SPEAKER_00]: This means I have the wrong view.

214
00:17:32,357 --> 00:17:35,981
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason... that's why I can suffer.

215
00:17:36,101 --> 00:17:41,026
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's therefore like a direct criteria even to be able to see.

216
00:17:41,998 --> 00:17:47,701
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not depending on what anybody else tells you or what any, you know, what you hear in the videos or from anywhere.

217
00:17:48,662 --> 00:17:54,245
[SPEAKER_00]: That this criteria means that, okay, there is there is wrong view there.

218
00:17:55,506 --> 00:17:59,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Which also means then you have to, yeah, admit that you suffer.

219
00:17:59,128 --> 00:18:02,230
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like recognize when you when you are affected by things.

220
00:18:02,250 --> 00:18:11,375
[SPEAKER_04]: And what's, eh... What is a necessary basis for people being able to honestly, transparently, authentically admit

221
00:18:13,270 --> 00:18:15,651
[SPEAKER_04]: There they suffer, and they're within suffering.

222
00:18:16,211 --> 00:18:21,772
[SPEAKER_04]: Which is kind of a prerequisite to be able to then relate to the practice of the Right View correctly.

223
00:18:22,392 --> 00:18:25,293
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what is the prerequisite?

224
00:18:25,313 --> 00:18:28,613
[SPEAKER_04]: What's a necessary basis for people to be able to admit that correctly?

225
00:18:29,274 --> 00:18:31,514
[SPEAKER_04]: Because people might admit that, but still kind of move on.

226
00:18:32,174 --> 00:18:40,276
[SPEAKER_04]: As in, yeah, but see, what I'm doing is helping, I find this helpful, I find this calming, I find all this... What do all those things have in common, if you haven't admitted truly?

227
00:18:42,278 --> 00:18:42,758
[SPEAKER_03]: Try to manage.

228
00:18:43,158 --> 00:18:43,498
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

229
00:18:43,698 --> 00:18:44,238
[SPEAKER_04]: Management.

230
00:18:44,358 --> 00:18:46,979
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you have to give up the management of suffering.

231
00:18:47,199 --> 00:18:48,219
[SPEAKER_04]: So, wanting to change.

232
00:18:49,119 --> 00:18:51,400
[SPEAKER_04]: Acting out of it, wanting to change, thinking you can deal with it.

233
00:18:51,740 --> 00:19:00,261
[SPEAKER_04]: You need to fully feel enclosed within the existing dukkha of any kind, without giving in to, okay, I'll deal with this.

234
00:19:01,182 --> 00:19:07,983
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the prerequisite for then seeing the craving as the presently enduring basis for the presently enduring suffering.

235
00:19:08,943 --> 00:19:23,089
[SPEAKER_04]: For as long as you're still holding on a thread of, I can do this, this, this, this, I can calm nostril sensations, this, when it's like this, when it's a soup, I look at soup, I look at soup... All of these managements are still the reason why you won't have the right view.

236
00:19:23,589 --> 00:19:26,150
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you're not giving up, you're dealing with the pain.

237
00:19:26,951 --> 00:19:28,912
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in other words, you're not uprooting it.

238
00:19:29,552 --> 00:19:34,374
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in a sense, more specifically, one needs to admit that he doesn't know why he suffers.

239
00:19:35,401 --> 00:19:35,981
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

240
00:19:36,021 --> 00:19:44,345
[SPEAKER_04]: One needs to admit that he doesn't know why he suffers, and then one needs to restrain himself from managing the suffering, because that's a habit.

241
00:19:45,065 --> 00:19:47,946
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not anymore even on the level of why I do this.

242
00:19:48,006 --> 00:19:49,306
[SPEAKER_04]: It's more like I just do this.

243
00:19:50,087 --> 00:19:55,149
[SPEAKER_04]: And then the dhamma, the practice, the talks, the suttas, became the extension of your management.

244
00:19:56,029 --> 00:20:00,691
[SPEAKER_04]: So, people usually manage suffering through distraction, sensuality, acting out of ill will,

245
00:20:01,677 --> 00:20:10,303
[SPEAKER_04]: As the Buddha said, you know, Kutujana does not know any other escape from the arisen painful feeling other than seeking pleasure in sense objects.

246
00:20:10,543 --> 00:20:18,829
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, if somebody says, okay, that's bad, I'll practice meditation and spirituality and the dhamma and the suttas, but if they do it with the same attitude.

247
00:20:20,010 --> 00:20:23,553
[SPEAKER_04]: So, they turn to it to get rid of the pain, to manage the pain.

248
00:20:24,783 --> 00:20:28,425
[SPEAKER_04]: And that won't be the dhamma, even if it's all they do is stuff that's what they say.

249
00:20:29,065 --> 00:20:34,647
[SPEAKER_04]: Because they're doing it on the basis of their aversion, on the basis of their resistance, on the basis of their craving.

250
00:20:36,508 --> 00:20:42,250
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, a person can say, okay, so I was given essentiality, now I'm just sort of managing the suffering.

251
00:20:42,990 --> 00:20:51,254
[SPEAKER_04]: I need to really refrain myself from giving into the pressure to manage, explain, psychologize,

252
00:20:51,973 --> 00:20:56,836
[SPEAKER_04]: Practice out, calm through my meditation, whatever else, of the arisen pressure in my mind.

253
00:20:58,837 --> 00:21:02,339
[SPEAKER_04]: I need to learn how to endure it, without craving against it.

254
00:21:03,759 --> 00:21:08,402
[SPEAKER_04]: And when the craving against it ceases, the pain of it ceases, while the thing still remains.

255
00:21:08,522 --> 00:21:09,903
[SPEAKER_04]: So look, amid suffering,

256
00:21:11,052 --> 00:21:18,059
[SPEAKER_04]: Amidst the same thing that pressures you, the suffering has ceased, because the craving against it has ceased.

257
00:21:18,079 --> 00:21:18,640
[SPEAKER_04]: Not the thing.

258
00:21:18,960 --> 00:21:19,481
[SPEAKER_04]: It's still there.

259
00:21:20,141 --> 00:21:21,763
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's like, oh, I got rid of it.

260
00:21:22,403 --> 00:21:25,386
[SPEAKER_04]: I got rid of the problem of craving.

261
00:21:26,367 --> 00:21:31,412
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't need to get rid of the things you never did, including your own thoughts.

262
00:21:32,714 --> 00:21:33,615
[SPEAKER_04]: They were not the issue.

263
00:21:37,748 --> 00:21:55,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to back up slightly, the... when you were talking about the needing to restrain any kind of management, I think part of being able to even, you know, to do that, is also even just to

264
00:21:56,031 --> 00:22:08,022
[SPEAKER_00]: You really need to take responsibility for the management that you are doing, in the sense that you can do it because you think so-and-so told you that it was the right way, or you read it there.

265
00:22:08,042 --> 00:22:17,550
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to really know that you are the one doing it, and you are fully responsible for any view about the practice of dhamma that you are holding.

266
00:22:18,211 --> 00:22:23,055
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, regardless of where it comes from, or on what basis you hold it, there is no

267
00:22:24,465 --> 00:22:30,950
[SPEAKER_00]: The particular reason to hold any view about what the dhamma is, or what the practice is, you're choosing to do it.

268
00:22:30,990 --> 00:22:41,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So, only by taking responsibility for it fully, can you then, you know, see that, oh, I'm doing this out of craving, or I'm doing it just because I'm doing it.

269
00:22:41,719 --> 00:22:43,580
[SPEAKER_00]: So, therefore, you can restrain yourself.

270
00:22:44,201 --> 00:22:46,783
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're not taking responsibility for that from the beginning,

271
00:22:47,523 --> 00:22:49,045
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just going to perpetuate itself.

272
00:22:49,345 --> 00:22:52,168
[SPEAKER_04]: Your management attempts and avoidance and so on.

273
00:22:52,208 --> 00:22:52,669
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

274
00:22:52,769 --> 00:22:59,937
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no way to restrain it properly or to even realize that you're doing it in order to restrain it.

275
00:23:00,778 --> 00:23:00,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

276
00:23:01,119 --> 00:23:06,184
[SPEAKER_00]: And that can be like on the basis of faith, on the basis of

277
00:23:08,228 --> 00:23:21,413
[SPEAKER_00]: What you interpret the text, what you... whatever, but you have to realize that you're the one having the faith, you're the one interpreting the text, you're the one... there's no particular... there's nothing that makes you do it.

278
00:23:22,193 --> 00:23:22,433
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

279
00:23:23,834 --> 00:23:35,258
[SPEAKER_04]: So, practically then, how do you... or if somebody can think of some examples, how do you not... how do you refrain from management?

280
00:23:36,497 --> 00:23:38,498
[SPEAKER_04]: Or what happens if you refrain from management?

281
