370
00:28:37,110 --> 00:28:37,991
[SPEAKER_04]: That's kind of the point.

371
00:28:39,083 --> 00:28:40,524
[SPEAKER_04]: And initially, it might take quite long.

372
00:28:40,724 --> 00:28:41,124
[SPEAKER_04]: Same thing.

373
00:28:41,585 --> 00:28:43,186
[SPEAKER_04]: Same thing can last for four or five days.

374
00:28:43,466 --> 00:28:44,747
[SPEAKER_04]: Just be on your mind.

375
00:28:44,787 --> 00:28:45,227
[SPEAKER_04]: Same thing.

376
00:28:46,068 --> 00:28:46,708
[SPEAKER_04]: And you don't care.

377
00:28:47,448 --> 00:28:50,510
[SPEAKER_04]: In a sense, like, for as long as it lasts, you're gonna do it.

378
00:28:50,610 --> 00:28:51,231
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the point.

379
00:28:52,051 --> 00:28:54,713
[SPEAKER_04]: Because sometimes people feel like, okay, well, I'll do this for a week now.

380
00:28:54,733 --> 00:28:56,214
[SPEAKER_04]: It's time to... No.

381
00:28:57,115 --> 00:28:57,255
No.

382
00:28:58,275 --> 00:29:01,057
[SPEAKER_04]: It's never time to step into the domain that doesn't belong to you.

383
00:29:01,978 --> 00:29:05,220
[SPEAKER_04]: Because the moment you do, you're back into Mara's domain, Mara's control.

384
00:29:07,388 --> 00:29:09,309
[SPEAKER_04]: But it is going to last forever, for us, yeah.

385
00:29:10,289 --> 00:29:12,230
[SPEAKER_04]: Māra's domain will be Māra's domain forever.

386
00:29:12,671 --> 00:29:13,371
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the whole point.

387
00:29:14,111 --> 00:29:15,072
[SPEAKER_04]: And there was never the problem.

388
00:29:16,752 --> 00:29:24,416
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the problem is that you're just pressured to grab, hook onto the bait, because you can't bear the presence of it, the pressure of it.

389
00:29:26,737 --> 00:29:36,882
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if there is the imminent danger to losing the sensory strength, compromising virtue by body, speech, physically, right here, then yes, of course.

390
00:29:38,027 --> 00:29:38,508
[SPEAKER_04]: Avoid that.

391
00:29:39,669 --> 00:29:40,590
[SPEAKER_04]: Wisely avoid it.

392
00:29:42,952 --> 00:29:46,636
[SPEAKER_04]: But if there isn't one, then you have to endure it.

393
00:29:47,437 --> 00:29:54,785
[SPEAKER_04]: Because otherwise, you're just going to be self-perpetuating management and trying to keep it and basically fueling your craving because of which you suffer and you're trying to manage.

394
00:29:56,580 --> 00:30:00,523
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't manage things, or you don't need to manage things, because they bother you.

395
00:30:00,864 --> 00:30:02,525
[SPEAKER_04]: You manage things because you crave against them.

396
00:30:03,206 --> 00:30:05,628
[SPEAKER_04]: And your management against them is another form of craving.

397
00:30:05,928 --> 00:30:09,511
[SPEAKER_04]: So, your management will just result in more need for management.

398
00:30:15,096 --> 00:30:20,460
[SPEAKER_04]: And same, like, for people, you know, they get ill or something, and they're like, it's unpleasant, there's pressure in my mind.

399
00:30:20,800 --> 00:30:22,982
[SPEAKER_04]: But do we go to see the doctor that's acting out of craving?

400
00:30:23,022 --> 00:30:23,483
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, it is.

401
00:30:25,045 --> 00:30:26,226
[SPEAKER_04]: But is it against the precepts?

402
00:30:26,346 --> 00:30:26,566
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

403
00:30:28,787 --> 00:30:30,688
[SPEAKER_04]: If you're cured, can you then practice better?

404
00:30:30,808 --> 00:30:31,128
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

405
00:30:31,668 --> 00:30:33,009
[SPEAKER_04]: If you don't cure yourself, could you die?

406
00:30:33,189 --> 00:30:33,509
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

407
00:30:34,310 --> 00:30:35,530
[SPEAKER_04]: So, then go to the doctor.

408
00:30:35,990 --> 00:30:36,711
[SPEAKER_04]: Act out of craving.

409
00:30:36,851 --> 00:30:38,272
[SPEAKER_04]: Take responsibility for that.

410
00:30:38,812 --> 00:30:40,973
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you're not compromising sensory strength in precepts.

411
00:30:41,553 --> 00:30:45,155
[SPEAKER_04]: You're on the mental level you gave in, just so that then you can practice further.

412
00:30:48,353 --> 00:30:59,708
[SPEAKER_04]: But that doesn't mean now that, when there is no illness, no imminent danger, or pressure, or threat to your sensory strength, you're sitting in your room, and any discomfort, you still feel justified in managing it, mentally.

413
00:30:59,848 --> 00:30:59,988
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

414
00:31:00,269 --> 00:31:01,270
[SPEAKER_04]: Now that's overdoing it.

415
00:31:01,911 --> 00:31:06,056
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's like certain things, the Buddha said, should be wisely avoided.

416
00:31:07,185 --> 00:31:11,546
[SPEAKER_04]: Like the wild dog, an elephant, charcoal pit, don't just overlie out of craving.

417
00:31:12,006 --> 00:31:14,867
[SPEAKER_04]: If I turn away from a wild elephant, that would be rooted in craving.

418
00:31:15,007 --> 00:31:15,927
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm attached to my life.

419
00:31:16,827 --> 00:31:18,688
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, I'll just go and let the elephant kill me.

420
00:31:18,748 --> 00:31:24,109
[SPEAKER_04]: It was like, yeah, but you are still factually not enlightened, so you're gonna die and lose the opportunity to practice.

421
00:31:24,609 --> 00:31:28,090
[SPEAKER_04]: So, use some common sense, basically.

422
00:31:28,850 --> 00:31:36,552
[SPEAKER_04]: Avoid the elephant, admit the character of craving and weakness, and then practice before the next elephant comes, before the next illness comes.

423
00:31:37,953 --> 00:31:52,739
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the criteria of, basically, practice is the criteria for, like, action.

424
00:31:52,779 --> 00:31:58,861
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's say I have a duty imposed on me by the community that I live in.

425
00:32:04,074 --> 00:32:07,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I could just not do it or refuse to do it.

426
00:32:07,937 --> 00:32:09,718
[SPEAKER_01]: But that would be...

427
00:32:10,138 --> 00:32:12,039
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, like this example of going to the doctor.

428
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:15,402
[SPEAKER_01]: You go there to cure your disease so you can practice.

429
00:32:16,682 --> 00:32:20,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's less hassle to fulfill your duty.

430
00:32:21,586 --> 00:32:22,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

431
00:32:22,927 --> 00:32:23,767
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can practice.

432
00:32:23,907 --> 00:32:29,371
[SPEAKER_04]: But there is also a line now, see, where the... how far the duty goes.

433
00:32:34,429 --> 00:32:38,370
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, just adopt a view that whenever something bothers you, you're justified to go to adopt it.

434
00:32:38,390 --> 00:32:38,670
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

435
00:32:39,311 --> 00:32:41,731
[SPEAKER_04]: And equally, somebody says also, I must perform a duty.

436
00:32:41,871 --> 00:32:43,712
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, yeah, but it depends on the duties as well.

437
00:32:43,772 --> 00:32:53,355
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that requires... Do you live in the community where duties imposed on you are of a more or less practical kind of necessity?

438
00:32:54,155 --> 00:32:57,236
[SPEAKER_04]: Or are they just random sort of decisions by random people?

439
00:32:57,256 --> 00:32:59,717
[SPEAKER_04]: They have nothing to do with any kind of practical benefit.

440
00:32:59,777 --> 00:33:03,558
[SPEAKER_04]: It was just to fulfill time or ideas or fantasies or whatever else.

441
00:33:06,403 --> 00:33:14,947
[SPEAKER_04]: you know, sweeping, preparing food, repairing, maintenance, so then you can live in a degree of, kind of, not too uncomfortable, and so on.

442
00:33:15,947 --> 00:33:19,669
[SPEAKER_04]: Or is your duty revolving around spending hours of just, you know, chatting to people,

443
00:33:20,938 --> 00:33:25,841
[SPEAKER_04]: dusting shrines, polishing statues and stuff, that has no practical value whatsoever.

444
00:33:26,522 --> 00:33:30,944
[SPEAKER_04]: Yet, it's equally time-consuming and requiring company and so on.

445
00:33:31,024 --> 00:33:33,886
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there is a different value to duties as well.

446
00:33:33,986 --> 00:33:36,208
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, it doesn't mean any duty should be performed.

447
00:33:37,068 --> 00:33:41,172
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, is your... is performing, like in the Sutta, we talk about performing duty towards your teacher.

448
00:33:41,372 --> 00:33:43,895
[SPEAKER_04]: So, helping him, because he's teaching you.

449
00:33:43,975 --> 00:33:45,596
[SPEAKER_04]: So, because you're already a burden.

450
00:33:45,656 --> 00:33:47,498
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, okay, so you take him on as a burden.

451
00:33:47,538 --> 00:33:51,862
[SPEAKER_04]: So, just cleaning around each scuti and so on, in return, yeah.

452
00:33:52,175 --> 00:33:54,958
[SPEAKER_04]: You hear the dhamma, you get your questions answered.

453
00:33:54,998 --> 00:33:55,798
[SPEAKER_04]: That's a fair deal.

454
00:33:56,539 --> 00:33:59,522
[SPEAKER_04]: Or, you don't even know what your teacher understands or not.

455
00:34:00,163 --> 00:34:09,391
[SPEAKER_04]: You just took him out of the institutional value of nisaya, that monks must do, and then you're full-on 24-7 servant, just for the sake of it.

456
00:34:10,172 --> 00:34:14,636
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, well, that's not wise, isn't it?

457
00:34:14,656 --> 00:34:18,460
[SPEAKER_04]: So, just, again, common sense is a practical need for this.

458
00:34:19,502 --> 00:34:25,244
[SPEAKER_03]: This great duty or project that community does, is it necessary in that sense?

459
00:34:25,944 --> 00:34:28,425
[SPEAKER_04]: Or is it just because somebody, oh, I like it.

460
00:34:28,485 --> 00:34:29,145
[SPEAKER_04]: I would like this.

461
00:34:29,706 --> 00:34:34,667
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you please, all of you, spend 10 hours a day doing, you know, building this thing for me, because I like it.

462
00:34:35,728 --> 00:34:36,748
[SPEAKER_04]: Because I would like to have it.

463
00:34:37,448 --> 00:34:39,529
[SPEAKER_04]: Because I like all the ideas that revolve around it.

464
00:34:40,429 --> 00:34:43,410
[SPEAKER_04]: Or do you like, oh, no, spend 10 hours fixing the water pipe.

465
00:34:43,630 --> 00:34:45,331
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if you don't do it, we won't have water.

466
00:34:47,335 --> 00:34:48,876
[SPEAKER_03]: And then things will get worse in other ways.

467
00:34:49,076 --> 00:34:51,637
[SPEAKER_03]: You get sick and then you'll have to go to the doctors and so on.

468
00:34:52,078 --> 00:34:55,740
[SPEAKER_04]: So, like, there is, again, common sense that would denominate.

469
00:34:56,040 --> 00:34:56,881
[SPEAKER_04]: Is this worth doing?

470
00:34:57,001 --> 00:34:57,942
[SPEAKER_04]: Wasting my time with?

471
00:34:58,022 --> 00:35:01,904
[SPEAKER_04]: Because both are, yeah, equally can become distracting and wasting time.

472
00:35:04,006 --> 00:35:06,007
[SPEAKER_04]: But, yeah, some of it is necessary to some extent.

473
00:35:06,487 --> 00:35:10,330
[SPEAKER_04]: So, but when people accept the duty at face value,

474
00:35:11,589 --> 00:35:19,552
[SPEAKER_04]: Means, okay, so any context, any discernment of practical necessity, need, benefit, is gone.

475
00:35:19,712 --> 00:35:27,495
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just done because it's a duty, determined by the higher authority of the tradition, community, senior monk or whatever else.

476
00:35:27,555 --> 00:35:29,616
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's certainly not the duty you should be following.

477
00:35:33,417 --> 00:35:34,798
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, you need to change community.

478
00:35:36,578 --> 00:35:38,219
[SPEAKER_03]: Or go on your own, if you know what you're doing.

479
00:35:41,602 --> 00:35:45,908
[SPEAKER_04]: Or try and talk to the community and see if it can be sort of fixed.

480
00:35:47,130 --> 00:35:54,199
[SPEAKER_04]: Because sometimes maybe people lose perspective, but then they can be reminded and then things can kind of be restored, you know, in a practical order.

481
00:35:54,400 --> 00:35:55,762
[SPEAKER_04]: So it's some duty, but again...

482
00:35:56,747 --> 00:36:01,550
[SPEAKER_04]: But if it cannot be restored, if the duty is taken, the value of duty is taken as a face value, you must perform it.

483
00:36:01,590 --> 00:36:03,010
[SPEAKER_04]: Don't ask anybody any questions.

484
00:36:03,030 --> 00:36:03,771
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the tradition.

485
00:36:03,811 --> 00:36:04,551
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the ajan.

486
00:36:05,151 --> 00:36:06,072
[SPEAKER_04]: That's our routine.

487
00:36:06,412 --> 00:36:08,353
[SPEAKER_04]: Keep doing this for the sake of doing it.

488
00:36:08,473 --> 00:36:09,494
[SPEAKER_04]: There is no discussion there.

489
00:36:09,554 --> 00:36:12,195
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you should not live there, if you want to practice for the right view.

490
00:36:16,097 --> 00:36:19,839
[SPEAKER_04]: So, as I said in other talks, it's always just like, again, am I doing too much?

491
00:36:19,899 --> 00:36:21,460
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just practical questions.

492
00:36:22,120 --> 00:36:22,601
[SPEAKER_04]: Ask yourself.

493
00:36:22,741 --> 00:36:25,062
[SPEAKER_04]: How much time I spend in solitude during the day?

494
00:36:26,427 --> 00:36:28,048
[SPEAKER_04]: How much time I spend in company?

495
00:36:28,068 --> 00:36:33,893
[SPEAKER_04]: How much of that company time is due to my own choices and intentions and volunteering and jumping up at the opportunity?

496
00:36:34,453 --> 00:36:37,336
[SPEAKER_04]: How much is because it was duty imposed on me, unavoidable?

497
00:36:38,837 --> 00:36:46,143
[SPEAKER_04]: And once you clarify how much is on you, how much is not on you, then look at how much is not on you and whether that's a problem or not.

498
00:36:47,964 --> 00:36:51,227
[SPEAKER_04]: Because sometimes people say, oh, it's just so much, because they're going around doing everything.

499
00:36:51,667 --> 00:36:52,408
[SPEAKER_04]: Nobody asked them to.

500
00:36:53,379 --> 00:36:55,800
[SPEAKER_04]: So, like, there is a degree of personal responsibility there.

501
00:36:55,820 --> 00:37:05,444
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you have to first recognize that and then see that what... when... what... what's left... oh, but it's still like for six, seven hours I have to be... okay, now that's too much.

502
00:37:05,484 --> 00:37:08,466
[SPEAKER_04]: So, then you can try and rectify that one way or the other.

503
00:37:09,486 --> 00:37:10,767
[SPEAKER_04]: Because solitude is necessary.

504
00:37:11,387 --> 00:37:13,308
[SPEAKER_04]: You know, we started this... how do you get the right view?

505
00:37:13,428 --> 00:37:13,648
[SPEAKER_04]: Well,

506
00:37:16,495 --> 00:37:22,159
[SPEAKER_04]: Virtue, taking the... seeing the danger in the slightest fault, slightest acting out, sense restraint.

507
00:37:22,459 --> 00:37:25,701
[SPEAKER_04]: So, oh, it's just... it's just a look, it's just a sound.

508
00:37:25,921 --> 00:37:26,081
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

509
00:37:26,982 --> 00:37:36,407
[SPEAKER_04]: It's important to... to sustain the value of, no, I mustn't give in to this and do the precious without... without giving in to the management of it.

510
00:37:37,988 --> 00:37:38,709
[SPEAKER_04]: Use common sense.

511
00:37:39,991 --> 00:37:47,635
[SPEAKER_04]: Manage if you need to go to a doctor or go to a more quiet place in the room and so on, but not for the sake of avoiding things that you know you have to deal with internally.

512
00:37:48,715 --> 00:37:49,595
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, seclusion.

513
00:37:50,296 --> 00:37:59,500
[SPEAKER_04]: You need to be dwelling in seclusion until this peripheral, the signs of the mind become apparent, where the mind is, the state of mind enduring on its own for what it is.

514
00:37:59,540 --> 00:38:02,641
[SPEAKER_04]: Because without being alone, that won't be seen.

515
00:38:02,822 --> 00:38:08,184
[SPEAKER_04]: Because all you see is yourself through others and others through your sense of self, whatever way perversion is there, of order.

516
00:38:10,667 --> 00:38:13,548
[SPEAKER_04]: But in solitude, that gets unperverted.

517
00:38:14,528 --> 00:38:22,651
[SPEAKER_04]: With this basis of gradual training set up, the solitude will unpervert the... you putting your sense of self first, and then you just see your mind there first.

518
00:38:23,911 --> 00:38:25,252
[SPEAKER_04]: Body there first.

519
00:38:25,292 --> 00:38:27,372
[SPEAKER_04]: Any of these right perceptions there first.

520
00:38:28,332 --> 00:38:31,533
[SPEAKER_04]: And then your sense of self enclosed within that, which means it's not your own anymore.

521
00:38:31,914 --> 00:38:33,594
[SPEAKER_04]: So then your atta becomes anatta.

522
00:38:34,854 --> 00:38:36,995
[SPEAKER_04]: Atta still there, just now seen a second.

523
00:38:37,355 --> 00:38:39,416
[SPEAKER_04]: And if atta is second, it's not atta anymore.

524
00:38:41,571 --> 00:38:42,832
[SPEAKER_04]: It's there which doesn't belong to you.

525
00:38:50,019 --> 00:38:52,021
[SPEAKER_04]: And that would kind of complete the third factor.

526
00:38:52,122 --> 00:39:00,530
[SPEAKER_04]: There will be abandonment with the right view, through these gradual practices we just described, in different way, but that's exactly what we're describing.

527
00:39:02,753 --> 00:39:07,917
[SPEAKER_00]: I could again just back up slightly, even just because that very first thing even that you said.

528
00:39:09,058 --> 00:39:13,361
[SPEAKER_00]: So, being restrained and seeing the danger in the slightest fault.

529
00:39:16,243 --> 00:39:25,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even that, for example, you should not necessarily take for granted, from the beginning, what you think a slightest fault is.

530
00:39:26,671 --> 00:39:28,953
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you know, somebody might say,

531
00:39:30,633 --> 00:39:39,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and I know that these precepts and so on, I'm supposed to be content with that, but that's not enough for me.

532
00:39:39,821 --> 00:39:47,688
[SPEAKER_00]: I also need to do these... add various other kind of rules or whatever, in order to not feel guilty or so.

533
00:39:47,708 --> 00:39:48,949
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, yeah, I think that's...

534
00:39:49,530 --> 00:39:52,913
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you will have... you will take... everybody will start with

535
00:39:54,299 --> 00:39:56,421
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, everything is a fault and so on.

536
00:39:56,441 --> 00:39:57,321
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't necessarily know.

537
00:39:57,341 --> 00:39:58,643
[SPEAKER_04]: So, common sense again.

538
00:39:58,663 --> 00:40:02,325
[SPEAKER_04]: So, danger in the slightest fault means the fault in regard to the precepts.

539
00:40:03,266 --> 00:40:06,529
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if the precept says, you know, don't lie.

540
00:40:07,289 --> 00:40:13,274
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so slightest fault regarding the principle of lying, not telling the truth, I should avoid.

541
00:40:14,095 --> 00:40:19,279
[SPEAKER_04]: Don't steal, don't kill, eight precepts, don't eat in the afternoon and so on.

542
00:40:19,379 --> 00:40:21,241
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so the slightest fault around that.

543
00:40:21,937 --> 00:40:27,280
[SPEAKER_04]: But, of course, the mind will then make fault out of things that are not necessarily faults or try to amend its own rules.

544
00:40:27,681 --> 00:40:30,022
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's when you're like, okay, so, but why am I doing this now?

545
00:40:31,063 --> 00:40:35,345
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you're going to have the pain of precepts, the restraint, the pain that's revealed on account of it.

546
00:40:36,526 --> 00:40:46,172
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, exactly, you can then double down on precepts and rules and imposing more observances and stuff that you do, because you want to manage the pain that there isn't of it.

547
00:40:47,073 --> 00:40:47,453
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, see,

548
00:40:49,612 --> 00:40:50,793
[SPEAKER_04]: It says don't eat in the afternoon.

549
00:40:51,353 --> 00:40:53,715
[SPEAKER_04]: And, eh... this might be allowable.

550
00:40:53,755 --> 00:40:54,415
[SPEAKER_04]: Maybe it isn't.

551
00:40:55,096 --> 00:40:55,576
[SPEAKER_04]: I ate it.

552
00:40:55,736 --> 00:40:56,577
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm doubtful now.

553
00:40:57,758 --> 00:41:00,300
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, you kind of find out that it actually was all right.

554
00:41:00,700 --> 00:41:04,903
[SPEAKER_04]: In certain instances, you can have certain types of food, even as our Vinaya allows.

555
00:41:05,843 --> 00:41:08,285
[SPEAKER_04]: But the doubt, the pain of it, the guilt is still there.

556
00:41:08,585 --> 00:41:09,426
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what do you do now?

557
00:41:10,207 --> 00:41:16,151
